ength wavering but her spirit  unbroken  ross lord global news halifax  sharing their stories ahead the vital  human rights tasks being done overseas  with the help of our journalists  as you may have noticed one global  national correspondent hasn't been on  air much lately and with good reason our  mike armstrong is in the democratic  republic of the congo working with  journalists for human rights a canadian  organization helping to train  journalists while promoting human rights  and gender equality here's a look at  what mike's been up to and what it's  meant to him  [Music]  this has been an experience  from the minute you leave the airport  and head downtown you realize kinshasa  is a little different  there's a battle they have about a  million times a day and here it is two  cars fighting over two inches  this isn't an easy place to do a lot of  things including reporting  over the last two months i've been  working with local journalists a few of  the newsrooms actually did stories on  the seminars reformatter michael  armstrong venus  i've also been in the field shooting and  editing stories with reporting teams  with saiyan tv i visited an orphanage  that might have to close that would put  these kids on the streets  with artesia we did a couple of stories  about a fishing village  and with digital congo we visited the  local downtown market to talk about the  same problem canadians are struggling  with inflation ah they're not happy  either  the goal has been to maybe hopefully  help if you want to have a healthy  democracy you tend to need a healthy  media so global kind of loaned two of  its journalists out to  journalists for human rights  my colleague rachel gilmore from our  online division has been working in  tunisia now jhr is based in toronto but  does work in several countries in africa  and the middle east they have all sorts  of programs promoting equality and human  rights  at its office in kinshasa last month it  was training journalists to become human  rights trainers themselves  and then two days later those trainers  delivered to other journalists  just in my time here there have been  meetings with women's rights groups  conferences with news directors  and a meeting with government officials  where jhr is trying to change the legal  system so that women who are victims of  sexual assault don't have to pay to  press charges  it's hard to compare problems we might  complain about in canada with the ones  people experience every day here  prices may be exploding but people in  this country are already starving about  30 million people here don't have enough  to eat  add to that violence from militias in  eastern parts of the country and  tensions with neighboring rwanda there  is real fear things could spiral out of  control  but through all of that i've met men and  women who work hard journalists who do  the job under conditions i've had  trouble believing  in newsrooms here computers are a luxury  even pens are hard to come by  and how about reading a live newscast  lights that never stop flickering  they do it all with dedication and  professionalism  now there is one thing i'll take home  with me when i leave this weekend it's  something i pick up in every country i  visit perspective a reminder that canada  may not be perfect  but we are absolutely  darn lucky  mike armstrong global news kinshasa the  democratic republic of the congo  from pests to heroes next how rats could  become critical critters  when disaster strikes we turn to dogs  for help with search and rescue  operations after all they're called  man's best friend for a reason  rats on the other hand have a bad rap  though as redmond shannon explains  researchers in africa are trying to turn  the rodents into rescuers  man's best friend may soon have some  smaller teammates on the search and  rescue squad  say hello to hero rats these rodents are  in training in tanzania to help  researchers locate and communicate with  earthquake victims how would that work  exactly let the researchers explain  typically with dogs they just go around  the perimeter of a debris site but with  their rats we're hoping they can  actually penetrate inside the debris and  get closer to victims  the rats will wear tiny backpacks kind  of like in paw patrol or batman's  utility belt it has a camera a precision  location transmitter and even a two-way  radio that rescuers could use to speak  with anyone trapped when they get close  to a human they'll trigger a switch  that's  integrated into this backpack  and that will give us  in theory a very precise location of  exactly where those victims are heading  of course it may not be for everyone you  can imagine a traumatized person buried  under rubble for hours or days may not  want to be suddenly confronted with a  rat so one thing that we've been  considering is that they might play a  message like an audio recording that  says something like i am a rescue rat  i'm well trained i'm here to help you  something along those lines the ngo of  popo has already trained rats for other  heroic missions such as sniffing out  landmines and even detecting  tuberculosis the goal is to eventually  deploy fully trained rats to earthquake  zones in turkey and one of them is  actually named after my nephew his name  is daniel  and so i'm very biased and he's one of  my favorites because he's top performing  but the main reason is that he's really  sweet and cuddly even if you don't agree  you can sponsor the program by adopting  a rat and potentially saving a life  remember not all heroes wear capes some  wear backpacks redmond shannon global  news london  to the very first hero in many of our  lives happy father's day  it is my first weekend as anchor of  global national and on a personal note i  wanted to thank the man who got me here  my dad and biggest fan came to canada as  an immigrant in the 70s he had no idea  that his daughter would one day anchor a  national newscast and for the past 22  years he's been sending me a note or  give me a phone call after every single  broadcast  he unknowingly reigns two journalists my  brother is also one and it's because of  his insatiable curiosity about the world  i mean he wake us up with news clippings  to teach us about politics and democracy  and he asks more questions than any  journalist i know happy father's day dad  we love you  that's global national for this sunday  night nito garcia will be with you  tomorrow until next time take care of  yourselves and each other good night  [Music]  you
 

